          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Plutarch</strong><br>\nPlutarch was born around 46 AD in Chaeronea, Boeotia, in ancient Greece. He was raised in a wealthy family, which allowed him access to a good education. From his writings, we can infer that Plutarch valued his upbringing and the opportunity to learn about philosophy and ethics from a young age. His family's status afforded him the privilege of traveling to study in Athens, the center of learning at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plutarch's Works</strong><br>\nPlutarch is most famous for his work \"Parallel Lives,\" a series of biographies of famous Greek and Roman men. In this work, he compared the lives of these figures, showing both their virtues and vices. He aimed to provide moral examples through their stories. Plutarch also wrote \"Moralia,\" a collection of essays and dialogues on ethical, religious, physical, political, and literary topics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plutarch as a Philosopher</strong><br>\nAs a philosopher, Plutarch was influenced by Platonic thought. He did not write formal philosophical treatises but conveyed his ideas through essays and lectures. He believed in the immortality of the soul and the importance of moral improvement. His works often explored the concept of virtue and how to live a good life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
