          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence of Plutarch's Ideas</strong><br>\nPlutarch's writings have had a lasting impact on both ancient and modern literature. His biographies were used by Shakespeare as sources for several plays. Plutarch's ideas on character and virtue influenced later thinkers and writers who sought to understand the complexities of human behavior and morality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plutarch's Role in Rome</strong><br>\nIn addition to his philosophical and literary contributions, Plutarch took on roles in public affairs. He served as a priest at the temple of Apollo in Delphi, where he was involved in various religious duties. His social status and connections also allowed him to travel to Rome, where he lectured on philosophy and enjoyed the company of influential figures.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plutarch's Method of Teaching</strong><br>\nPlutarch's teachings were characterized by his use of examples over abstract theorizing. He believed learning came from discussing real-life situations rather than philosophical conjectures. His lectures and writings were practical, aiming to be useful for people's daily lives and ethical decision-making.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
