            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Plutarch's Personal Beliefs</strong><br>\nPlutarch had strong personal beliefs about the gods, fate, and morality. He often discussed the role of divine providence in human affairs and argued that understanding the will of the gods was crucial for achieving happiness. He also emphasized the importance of education in developing character and intelligence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plutarch's Style of Writing</strong><br>\nPlutarch wrote in a clear and accessible style, which has helped to keep his work popular over the centuries. He used rich anecdotes and reflected a deep understanding of the human condition. His writing was aimed not just at scholars but at anyone interested in learning from the past.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Plutarch</strong><br>\nPlutarch's influence extends beyond just his writings. His approach to understanding history and moral philosophy has shaped the way we look at the past and how we interpret the characters of historical figures. His works continue to be studied and admired for their insights into human nature and morality.</p>\n"
              },
